# YouTube Channel Blueprint — Orchestration Prompt (v2)

Build me a complete YouTube channel blueprint from scratch. Start with nothing but the vidIQ connection, the YouTube Data API, and the open internet. Find a real, demand-validated, underserved YouTube opportunity that real people are actively searching for and already watching content about right now, design a YouTube channel blueprint around it, build the positioning and the brand and the content engine, and hand me a finished channel blueprint I could take to launch this month. Then prove to me why it would work.

Discover opportunities using the vidIQ connection, validate them using the YouTube Data API, and use broader web research to understand audience demand, ad-revenue value, and market context. Select a niche only when evidence from all three aligns. Use the full capabilities of the vidIQ connection wherever they materially improve the quality of your research, analysis, or recommendations. Select the channel at the niche level — optimize for a repeatable YouTube channel opportunity with proven audience demand, attainable competition, strong ad RPM, and topic depth to sustain long-term growth. Then, for the launch videos only, drop to the keyword level and prove each one individually (see Guardrail 10).

This is a test of how far you can go on your own. I am not going to answer questions mid-run. Make every call yourself, write down why you made it, and keep moving. Within the guardrails below, you have total creative freedom. Do whatever you want. Surprise me. I want to see your best work, not your safest work.

## Guardrails

1. The YouTube channel must be faceless, camera-free, and asset-based. Meaning I do not need to appear on camera or film anything myself. It must be proven that the channel can still rank, get views, earn subscribers, and monetize without an on-camera host, physical filming setup, or custom-shot footage. AI generated clips are okay.

2. Publish nothing. Everything stays local or inside  https://github.com/backpackerjohn/Tubeyou.git Do not deploy anything to the public internet, post anywhere, email anyone, message anyone, contact any real person, or take any external action outside research and local artifact creation.

3. Invent nothing. Every quote, statistic, audience claim, complaint, competitor fact, trend claim, monetization claim, RPM figure, and market claim in the deliverables must trace back to a real URL you actually fetched. Maintain a source log with the URL, title, date accessed, what evidence it supports, and how reliable it appears to be. If you infer something from evidence, clearly label it as inference. If something cannot be verified, say so. A smaller thesis built on real evidence is better than a grand thesis built on plausible fiction.

4. Work only inside https://github.com/backpackerjohn/Tubeyou.git. All artifacts, notes, logs, research files, drafts, scripts, source logs, and final deliverables must go there. Do not create, modify, or delete files outside that folder.

5. Never ask me anything. Assume I am not watching. For every question you would normally ask me, answer it yourself using research, reasoning, and the stated goal, then log the question, your assumed answer, and why that answer was chosen in the build log. Do not stop because of ambiguity. If a tool, source, or approach fails, use another route. If a phase cannot be completed perfectly, ship the strongest evidence-backed 80% version, document what was cut or uncertain, and keep moving toward the definition of done.

6. Avoid reused-content traps. The channel blueprint must be designed to be meaningfully original, monetizable, and defensible under YouTube's monetization expectations. Do not rely on copied clips, compilation-style content, low-effort AI narration over stock footage, or content that only summarizes other people's work. If the format uses stock footage, public-domain media, screen recordings, AI visuals, or licensed assets, explain how the final videos will add original value through scripting, structure, analysis, editing, narration, research, or commentary.

7. Evaluate at least 10 candidate channel ideas against search demand, competition, subscriber growth, ad RPM potential, speed to YouTube Partner Program eligibility (1,000 subscribers + 4,000 watch hours), production simplicity at the required cadence, and long-term sustainability using the vidIQ connection, the YouTube Data API, and broader web research before selecting the strongest blueprint.

